## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design scalable, multi-tenant streaming graph pipelines with strict data contracts, lineage, and compliance controls in modern lakehouse setups.

## Key Concepts

- Real-time streaming patterns: watermarking, lateness windows, fault tolerance
- Idempotent upserts in Iceberg with composite keys (tenant/device/neighbor)
- Schema evolution for nested attributes and backward compatibility
- Per-record lineage and auditability; GDPR delete handling via tombstones
- Multi-tenant isolation, access controls, and governance

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo upsert for edge state
function upsertEdge(existing, incoming) {
  const key = `${incoming.tenant_id}:${incoming.device_id}:${incoming.neighbor_id}`;
  if (!existing || existing.version < incoming.version) return incoming;
  return existing;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test late-arrival handling across tenants?
- What monitoring would you implement to detect cross-tenant data leakage or drift?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a practical, beginner-friendly end-to-end data pipeline with per-record contracts, canonical schemas, late data handling, and basic data quality reconciliation.

## Key Concepts

- Per-record contracts
- Lightweight schema registry
- Canonical schema and partitioning
- Watermarking for late data
- Data reconciliation and basic validation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Lightweight per-record contract check (pseudo)
function validate(record){
  if(!record) return false;
  const has=(k)=>record[k]!==undefined && record[k]!==null;
  return has('device_id') && has('event_ts') && has('metric');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate schema drift across regions?
- How would you adjust the test plan for larger spikes in regional feeds?
